[QA 1] The key contribution of the paper is introducing an exclusive style removal module that enhances cross-domain novel class discovery by removing domain-specific style information, thereby improving the performance of existing NCD methods on novel classes with different distributions. | The key contribution of this paper is proposing an exclusive style removal module to address the task of Cross Domain Novel Class Discovery (CDNCD), where the unlabeled data comes from novel classes and domains different from the labeled data. The paper provides theoretical analysis showing that removing the exclusive style information between labeled and unlabeled data is necessary for solving the CDNCD problem. Based on this, the authors introduce a style encoder trained in parallel with a baseline method to decouple the style and content features, enabling better alignment of content features for inference on novel classes across domains. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed module in improving performance on the CDNCD task compared to baseline methods.
