[QA 3] The paper presents a comprehensive numerical investigation of near-limit hydrogen-oxygen-nitrogen combustion in nonpremixed counterflow mixing layers, revealing a wide variety of flame dynamics and configurations that depend critically on stoichiometric mixture fraction and strain rate, challenging current theoretical expectations regarding the role of metallicity on wide binary formation. | The key contribution of this paper is characterizing the different combustion modes and flame structures that emerge in highly diluted counterflow hydrogen-oxygen flames with varying strain rates and mixture fractions near the crossover temperature using detailed numerical computations. The authors identify six different combustion regimes involving planar diffusion flames, advancing/retreating edge flames, multiple flame tubes, and isolated flame tubes, with complex dynamics and hysteresis effects. The variety of steady flame configurations, including a continuum of multiple flame tube arrays with different spacings and shapes, is remarkable and provides new insights into near-limit combustion phenomena.
